(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-endocrine-physiology-3-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Endocrine Physiology III: Reproductive Hormones — Quiz Set 2 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"GnRH is secreted in pulses at approximately what interval, driving LH/FSH release?","choices":["Continuously, with no pulsatile pattern","Once per menstrual cycle only","Every 1–3 hours","Once every 24 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"GnRH is secreted approximately every 1–3 hours, a pulsatile pattern that drives LH release (and testosterone production via LH's action on Leydig cells). — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Testosterone's effects on primary and secondary sexual characteristics include enlargement of the penis/scrotum/testes and:","choices":["Decreased muscle development, bone density, and red blood cell count","Decreased basal metabolic rate and decreased overall energy expenditure","Decreased red blood cell count and lowered hematocrit levels","Increased hair growth (axillary, chest, pubic, facial) and deepened voice"],"answer":3,"exp":"Testosterone increases hair growth (axillary, chest, pubic, facial) and causes voice deepening via laryngeal enlargement, alongside increased muscle development, bone density, and basal metabolic rate. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Testosterone's skeletal effects include increased bone thickness and calcium deposition, but prolonged exposure eventually causes:","choices":["Continuous linear bone growth that never plateaus, slows, or reaches any eventual final limit.","Fusion of the epiphyses/shaft, which can decrease final height if levels are abnormally high","Complete and permanent cessation of all bone mineralization activity throughout the skeleton.","Bones becoming progressively more radiolucent with no change in overall density."],"answer":1,"exp":"Prolonged testosterone exposure causes fusion of the bone epiphyses/shaft; abnormally high levels can cause this fusion to occur early, resulting in a net decrease in final height. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Testosterone's metabolic effect at puberty is to increase basal metabolic rate by approximately:","choices":["0.5–1%","50–100%","5–10%","No measurable change"],"answer":2,"exp":"Testosterone at puberty increases basal metabolic rate by about 5–10%, largely due to increased protein anabolism. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Increased metabolic rate from testosterone contributes to which hematologic change?","choices":["Decreased hematocrit due to more red blood cells","Decreased white blood cell count","Increased hematocrit due to more red blood cells","No effect on hematocrit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Testosterone's increased metabolic rate increases hematocrit, due to an increase in red blood cells. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which hormone stimulates testosterone production from Leydig cells, secreted every 1–3 hours from the hypothalamus?","choices":["Inhibin, a Sertoli cell product","Estrogen, a downstream product","LH, downstream of pulsatile GnRH","FSH, acting on Sertoli cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"LH, released downstream of pulsatile GnRH (secreted every 1–3 hours), stimulates testosterone production from Leydig cells. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"During early puberty (about ages 9–12), what happens to LH and FSH levels?","choices":["They increase steadily","They spike once and then permanently shut off","They decrease steadily","They remain completely flat with no change"],"answer":0,"exp":"LH and FSH levels increase steadily starting around ages 9–12 as part of pubertal onset. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"FSH and LH accelerate growth of approximately how many primary follicles per month?","choices":["Over 100","6–12","20–30","1–2"],"answer":1,"exp":"FSH (and LH) accelerate growth of approximately 6–12 primary follicles per month. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What triggers ovulation and development of the corpus luteum?","choices":["A drop in FSH to zero","A steady, unchanging level of estrogen","Progesterone acting alone, with no LH involvement","An LH surge"],"answer":3,"exp":"An LH surge leads to ovulation and development of the corpus luteum. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"After ovulation, what do estrogen and progesterone from the corpus luteum do to GnRH/LH/FSH?","choices":["Stimulate them continuously","Have no effect on them","Only affect FSH, not LH or GnRH","Suppress them"],"answer":3,"exp":"Estrogen and progesterone from the corpus luteum suppress GnRH/LH/FSH during the luteal phase, preventing a new cycle from starting prematurely. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What happens hormonally that allows a new ovulatory cycle to begin?","choices":["The corpus luteum persists indefinitely without ever involuting or losing function","LH production permanently and completely stops, with no further cycles occurring.","Estrogen and progesterone rise further, extending suppression indefinitely","Estrogen and progesterone from the corpus luteum fall, relieving suppression of GnRH/LH/FSH"],"answer":3,"exp":"As the corpus luteum involutes, estrogen and progesterone levels fall, relieving the suppression on GnRH/LH/FSH and allowing a new cycle to begin. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Approximately 14 days after ovulation without fertilization, what happens to the endometrium?","choices":["It thickens indefinitely","It sloughs off (menstruation)","Nothing changes","It converts entirely into decidual tissue permanently"],"answer":1,"exp":"About 14 days after ovulation, if fertilization has not occurred, the endometrium sloughs off — menstruation. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) is secreted from which structure, and is detectable as early as how many days postovulation?","choices":["Maternal pituitary gland; detectable at around 1 day postovulation","Corpus luteum tissue; detectable at around 30 days postovulation","Fetal adrenal cortex tissue; detectable at around 20 weeks postovulation","Syncytial trophoblasts; detectable as early as 8 days postovulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"hCG is secreted from syncytial trophoblasts and is detectable as early as 8 days postovulation, peaking at 10–12 weeks. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What is hCG's major function regarding the corpus luteum in early pregnancy?","choices":["It gradually converts the corpus luteum directly into functioning placental tissue","It prevents involution of the corpus luteum and stimulates its estrogen/progesterone production","It has essentially no functional relationship to the corpus luteum at any point.","It causes immediate and rapid involution of the corpus luteum within days"],"answer":1,"exp":"hCG prevents involution of the corpus luteum (increasing its function about 2×) and stimulates estrogen/progesterone secretion from it, until the corpus luteum slowly involutes after 12 weeks as hCG declines. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Besides supporting the corpus luteum, hCG also inhibits uterine contractions caused by which hormone?","choices":["Cortisol","Relaxin","Prolactin","Oxytocin"],"answer":3,"exp":"hCG inhibits uterine contractions caused by oxytocin, helping maintain the pregnancy. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Placental estrogen is synthesized primarily from which precursor?","choices":["Progesterone produced earlier by the maternal corpus luteum","Androgens (DHEA, 16-hydroxy-DHEA) from maternal and fetal adrenal sources","Maternal cholesterol, converted directly without intermediate steps","Thyroxine released from the maternal thyroid gland into circulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Placental estrogen is synthesized primarily from androgens (DHEA and 16-hydroxy-DHEA), sourced from both maternal and fetal adrenal glands (with a large fetal adrenal cortex producing DHEA). — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Placental progesterone is synthesized primarily from:","choices":["Maternal cholesterol","Placental estrogen","Fetal androgens","hCG, a glycoprotein hormone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Placental progesterone is synthesized primarily from maternal cholesterol. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"One function of placental progesterone is to reduce uterine contractility, which helps to:","choices":["Induce labor prematurely","Stimulate immediate lactation","Prevent spontaneous abortion","Increase menstrual flow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Placental progesterone reduces uterine contractility to help prevent spontaneous abortion. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"During pregnancy, which anterior pituitary hormones are suppressed, while ACTH, TSH, and prolactin increase?","choices":["Growth hormone and ACTH","LH and FSH","TSH and prolactin","ACTH and TSH"],"answer":1,"exp":"LH and FSH are suppressed during pregnancy, while ACTH, TSH, and prolactin increase. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"During pregnancy, aldosterone approximately doubles, contributing to:","choices":["No change in fluid balance","Sodium and water loss","Only potassium retention, with no sodium effect","Sodium and water retention"],"answer":3,"exp":"A twofold increase in aldosterone during pregnancy contributes to sodium and water retention. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"During pregnancy, thyroxine can increase by up to what percentage, driven partly by hCG and human chorionic thyrotropin?","choices":["150%","5%","50%","300%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Thyroxine can increase by up to 50% during pregnancy, caused by hCG and human chorionic thyrotropin. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Relaxin, secreted from the corpus luteum and stimulated by hCG, has a possible role to:","choices":["Stimulate ovulation","Increase uterine contractility early in pregnancy","Constrict pelvic ligaments to stabilize the pelvis","Relax pelvic ligaments"],"answer":3,"exp":"Relaxin's possible role is to relax pelvic ligaments, in preparation for later stages of pregnancy/delivery. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Braxton-Hicks contractions are best described as:","choices":["Strong, rhythmic uterine contractions that are entirely identical to true active labor","Contractions that only occur during the second stage of labor","A reliable sign indicating that delivery will occur within the hour","Weak, slow, irritable uterine muscle contractions beginning about 1 month before labor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Braxton-Hicks contractions are weak, slow contractions reflecting uterine muscle irritability, beginning about 1 month before true labor. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"True labor has a circadian rhythm, with contractions peaking during which time window?","choices":["There is no time-of-day pattern","6 am to 9 am","Noon to 3 pm","Midnight to 5 am"],"answer":3,"exp":"True labor has a circadian rhythm, peaking between midnight and 5 am. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"In the four-stage labor framework, what characterizes Stage 3?","choices":["Cervical dilation and effacement of the cervix","Placental delivery (separation and expulsion of the placenta)","Fetal expulsion (delivery of the baby)","Uterine involution and postpartum hemostasis over weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Stage 3 of labor is placental delivery — separation and expulsion of the placenta. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"Cervical stretching during labor stimulates oxytocin secretion from which structure?","choices":["Supraoptic nuclei of the hypothalamus (via posterior pituitary release)","The adrenal medulla, via direct sympathetic nervous activation","The anterior pituitary, via releasing-hormone stimulation","The ovary itself, via local paracrine hormonal signaling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cervical stretching stimulates oxytocin secretion, with oxytocin originating from the supraoptic nuclei of the hypothalamus and released via the posterior pituitary. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Besides maternal oxytocin, parturition also involves fetal release of oxytocin, cortisol, and:","choices":["Insulin","Prostaglandins","Growth hormone only","Aldosterone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fetal release of oxytocin, cortisol, and prostaglandins all contribute to the parturition process. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which hormone, increasing starting at gestation week 5 from the anterior pituitary, directly promotes milk secretion?","choices":["Relaxin","hCG hormone","Prolactin","Oxytocin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Prolactin, from the anterior pituitary, increases starting at gestation week 5 and promotes milk secretion. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Colostrum, the first milk, differs from mature milk in that it:","choices":["Contains essentially no protein content at all, unlike mature breast milk","Is produced by the breast only after weaning begins","Has essentially the same content as milk but with no fat","Contains essentially no maternal antibodies at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Colostrum has essentially the same content as regular milk but contains no fat. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which hormones are described as contributing nutrients needed for milk production, alongside prolactin?","choices":["LH, FSH, and inhibin, suppressed during lactation","GH, cortisol, thyroid hormones, and insulin","Only estrogen and progesterone acting together","Only oxytocin, acting entirely alone in this role"],"answer":1,"exp":"GH, cortisol, thyroid hormones, and insulin all contribute to the nutrients needed for milk production, alongside prolactin's direct secretory role. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 86"}]};
